DSO-1527 – Productive Lifecycle Reporting Spike
Objective

Evaluate an approach to improve the accuracy of monthly Snyk vulnerability reporting by establishing a consistent production monitoring target.

The objective of this spike was to determine the best method for ensuring production vulnerabilities are consistently reported against a single production branch rather than multiple release branches.

Background

The existing Snyk monitoring process could create multiple monitored targets when production deployments originated from different release branches.

Example:

release/2026.07
release/2026.08
release/2026.09

This made monthly reporting more difficult because production vulnerabilities were distributed across multiple release branch targets.

Following discussions with the Application Security (AppSec) team, the preferred approach is to maintain a single standard production branch for each application.

Proposed Solution

Use a standard production branch (typically master) as the authoritative production monitoring target.

After every production deployment:

Merge the production release branch into master.
Execute a Snyk monitor scan against master.
Ensure the master target reflects the latest production code.
Manually assign or verify the Production lifecycle on the master target.

This provides one consistent production target for vulnerability reporting.

POC Implementation

The existing devops-test-service-scan pipeline was updated to support scanning a configurable Git branch.

A new runtime parameter was introduced:

parameters:
- name: branchRef
  displayName: Branch to Scan
  type: string
  default: refs/heads/production-test

The repository checkout was updated to use this parameter:

resources:
  repositories:
    - repository: client-onboarding-svc
      ref: ${{ parameters.branchRef }}

This allows the pipeline to scan either:

a release branch
the production branch (master)

without modifying the pipeline code.

Validation Performed

The proof of concept was validated using:

Existing devops-test-service-scan pipeline
client-onboarding-svc repository

Validation included:

Selecting different Git branches for scanning.
Verifying successful checkout of the selected branch.
Confirming the existing pipeline correctly derives the Snyk BranchIdentifier.
Confirming the existing pipeline correctly derives the Snyk BranchName.
Confirming scans against the production branch update the existing production monitoring target.
Final Workflow
Release Branch Created
        │
        ▼
Production Deployment
        │
        ▼
Merge Release Branch → Master
        │
        ▼
Run Snyk Monitor Scan Against Master
        │
        ▼
Master Target Updated
        │
        ▼
Verify / Apply Production Lifecycle (Manual)
Findings

The proof of concept produced the following findings:

The existing DevOps scan pipeline can support configurable branch scanning with minimal changes.
Introducing a branchRef parameter allows the same reusable pipeline to scan different Git branches without modifying pipeline logic.
The existing Snyk scan template already derives the correct BranchIdentifier and BranchName from the checked-out Git branch.
Using a single production branch (master) provides a consistent production monitoring target.
Monthly vulnerability reporting becomes simpler because production results are associated with one standard target rather than multiple release branches.
Based on discussions with the AppSec team, manual assignment of the Production lifecycle is sufficient for this implementation and lifecycle automation is not required as part of this spike.
Benefits
Single production monitoring target.
Improved monthly vulnerability reporting.
Simplified production vulnerability tracking.
Minimal changes to the existing DevOps pipeline.
Reusable implementation across multiple applications.
Reduced maintenance compared to managing multiple production release targets.
Operational Process

Following each production deployment:

Merge the production release branch into master.
Execute the Snyk monitor scan against master.
Verify the scan completed successfully.
Confirm the master target reflects the latest production code.
Apply or verify the Production lifecycle on the master target within Snyk.
Optionally remove obsolete release branch targets if they are no longer required for reporting.
